fact WorkItem(id: "WI-889", description: some(value: "A HOST-SUPPLIED `const` HAS NO DECLARATIVE CHANNEL IN ANY LANGUAGE, so `anthill.prelude.Float.{infinity, negativeInfinity, nan}` are keyed by HARDCODED QUALIFIED NAME in every backend that realizes them — and cpp-gen's `render_as_float_special` is the LAST of the four hand-written per-carrier tables WI-886 set out to delete.\n\nWHAT WI-886 DID AND WHY THIS SURVIVED IT. WI-886 fed cpp-gen's per-carrier host surface from `operation_map` data (`kb.host_op_mappings()` filtered to `lang == \"cpp\"`), deleting `render_as_math_call` (21 entries), `render_as_math_constant` (3), and the per-carrier half of `render_as_operator` (5). `render_as_float_special` (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs) could not follow: `infinity`/`negativeInfinity`/`nan` are `SymbolKind::Const` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill declares them as `const infinity: Float`, WI-084/proposal 039), and `build_host_op_mappings` (kb/load.rs) REFUSES a mapping whose name is not an OPERATION — deliberately, because WI-876 MEASURED that admitting other kinds made `operation_map { pt: \"ordered_compare\" }` over `entity pt(...)` register a comparison as the CONSTRUCTOR.\n\nSO THE GAP IS SYMMETRIC ACROSS HOSTS, not a cpp-gen wart. The rust runtime keys the same three by hardcoded qualified name too (`register_if_present(interp, \"anthill.prelude.Float.infinity\", float_infinity)`, eval/builtins.rs ~line 120, whose own comment notes a const's value source IS the builtin map because `force_const` reads `self.builtins`). Two backends, two hardcoded lists, one absent channel — which is exactly the shape WI-876 removed for operations and WI-886 for cpp codegen.\n\nWHAT A FIX LOOKS LIKE, none assumed. (a) A `const_map { infinity: \"...\" }` clause in a binding block, peer of `operation_map`, landing as an `anthill.realization.ConstMapping` fact — symmetric, and it costs a grammar rule plus a loader arm plus two readers. (b) WIDEN `operation_map` to accept a const and record the KIND in the fact, so one clause covers both; cheaper, but it re-opens exactly the hole WI-876's kind check closed unless the widening is by an explicit opt-in rather than by dropping the check. (c) Declare that a host-supplied const IS a nullary operation and delete the `const` spelling for these three — note `Float.pi`/`e`/`tau` ARE nullary operations already and reach both backends through `operation_map`, so the three IEEE specials are the ODD ONES OUT within their own sort; but WI-884 recorded that a nullary operation and a bare const differ at the CALL SITE (a `[simp]` head is an application, `in_bounds` writes the bare `minValue`), so this is not a rename.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: whatever is chosen must keep the WI-876 kind check's guarantee — a mapping must not be able to attach a host implementation to a symbol that is neither an operation nor a const (an entity constructor, a sort).\n\nACCEPTANCE: the three Float IEEE specials are realized from DATA in at least the rust and cpp backends, or the decision to keep them hardcoded is written down where `render_as_float_special` and `register_standard_builtins` define them, with the reason; `render_as_float_special` is deleted or its survival is justified in place; the WI-876 kind check still refuses a non-operation, non-const target (a test); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: WI-886, which references this ticket number from `render_as_float_special`'s doc comment."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T21:22:54Z"))
